I"�<p>Imagine asking an AI agent to fix three unrelated bugs in a repository. One agent can investigate them in sequence. Three agents can investigate them at the same time, then hand their patches to a reviewer. That works if the bugs are independent. If two agents change the same module, they may spend more time reconciling their edits than they saved by starting together.</p>

<p>General Reasoning’s Avalon-M1 experiments ask whether agents can learn when that trade is worth making. The training setup gives a team a common score, a way to communicate, and a deadline. The goal is to turn parallel model calls into useful progress before time runs out. <a href="https://www.gr.inc/blog/how-to-train-a-swarm">General Reasoning’s How to Train a Swarm</a></p>

<!--more-->

<h2 id="what-a-deadline-teaches">What a deadline teaches</h2>

<p>The recipe is called <strong>reinforcement learning from collective rewards</strong>, or RLCR. Avalon-M1 uses instances of one model policy in different roles. A lead agent can recruit specialists, and agents can use direct messages, shared channels, and a workspace. During training, the group’s outcome supplies one learning signal for the agents’ actions. A successful run can reinforce a useful handoff even though the training system gives that message no separate grade. <a href="https://www.gr.inc/avalonm1.pdf#page=9">Avalon-M1 technical report, methodology</a></p>

<p>The deadline changes which strategies can earn a higher score. If one team passes seven tests before the clock expires and another passes four, the first has more progress to show. If both pass seven, the time cap alone gives them the same score, even if one finished much earlier. The deadline rewards speed when it makes a difference to the work completed in the time available. <a href="https://www.gr.inc/blog/how-to-train-a-swarm">How to Train a Swarm, time limits</a></p>

<p>This only works if progress can be measured when a run stops. A codebase with a test suite supplies a natural checkpoint. A task scored only after a complete answer may give two unfinished runs the same zero even if one is close to a solution. General Reasoning uses environments that can score partial progress and lengthens the time limit during training, giving teams room to learn strategies whose initial coordination costs take time to recover. <a href="https://www.gr.inc/avalonm1.pdf#page=13">Avalon-M1 technical report, training horizons</a></p>

