<h2 id="what-avalon-m1-measured">What Avalon-M1 measured</h2>

<p>The November 2 article reports that a single agent took roughly four times as long as a team when given a comparable token budget. The technical report matches <strong>output tokens</strong> in this comparison. It reconstructs latency from each run’s event log, using estimated generation times, measured tool durations, and message dependencies. The estimate describes how much work could overlap under that timing model. Actual elapsed time on a serving system also depends on queueing and batching. <a href="https://www.gr.inc/blog/how-to-train-a-swarm">General Reasoning’s results</a> · <a href="https://www.gr.inc/avalonm1.pdf#page=59">Avalon-M1 technical report, latency methodology</a></p>

<p>More output tokens can explain some of the quality gains seen against a shorter single-agent run. When the report gives a single agent the team’s output-token budget, the two approaches trade places across benchmarks. That comparison helps isolate the question that interests me most. If both can get to a similar answer, how long does each take to get there? <a href="https://www.gr.inc/avalonm1.pdf#page=20">Avalon-M1 technical report, token-level comparison</a></p>

