<h2 id="the-team-score-leaves-a-credit-problem">The team score leaves a credit problem</h2>

<p>A shared score is easy to specify, but it leaves the trainer with limited information about what helped. Suppose a specialist identifies the real cause of a bug, another repeats the same search, and a third reviews the eventual patch. A single high score covers all three. Over many runs, the policy can pick up useful patterns, but the signal gets harder to interpret as teams grow and decisions stretch over more hours. The article leaves open whether coordination learned in small teams transfers to much larger ones. <a href="https://www.gr.inc/blog/how-to-train-a-swarm">General Reasoning’s open problems</a></p>

<p>In the bug-fixing example, the comparison I would want is one agent with more time to think, several independent agents whose results are selected at the end, and a communicating team. Give each the same time limit, then record test progress, output tokens, and how often agents block each other. Independent work can be enough when the fixes barely interact. The communicating team earns its place when one agent’s discovery changes what another can do before the deadline.</p>
